     1	"""`disco verify` — does YOUR configured model actually drive the loop?
     2	
     3	The strategic wedge of disco is *reliability on local / open-weight models*.
     4	The corollary is that a self-hoster needs to find out their specific model works
     5	**before** trusting it with a task — not discover mid-run that it can't emit a tool
     6	call. This command runs a small battery of capability checks against the persisted
     7	config + live endpoints and prints a pass/fail table:
     8	
     9	    config       — a driver model is assigned and has a base URL
    10	    completion   — the driver endpoint is reachable and returns text
    11	    tool-calling — the driver emits a structured tool call when asked (THE gate:
    12	                   a model that can't tool-call cannot drive the agent loop)
    13	    grounding    — the full research pipeline returns a cited, NLI-verified answer
    14	                   (network + encoder download required; SKIPs cleanly if offline)
    15	
    16	Each check is PASS / FAIL / SKIP with the real reason (a provider's verbatim error,
    17	never a flattened one). The process exits non-zero iff any check FAILED (a SKIP is
    18	not a failure — it means "couldn't run here", e.g. no internet for grounding).
    19	
    20	Run it:
    21	    # in a compose deployment
    22	    docker compose exec agent-server python -m disco.agent_server.verify
    23	    # from a checkout
    24	    uv run python -m disco.agent_server.verify [--quick] [--no-network]
    25	
    26	This reuses the SAME router/provider/grounding composition the live loop uses
    27	(ConversationRuntime._router_now, retrieval.wiring.research_answer) — so a pass here
    28	is evidence about the real pipeline, not a toy.
    29	"""
    30	
    31	from __future__ import annotations
