   447	/**
   448	 * Split command handling into two planes so a hung in-flight `prompt()` can never
   449	 * wedge cancellation or shutdown:
   450	 *
   451	 *  - DATA plane: `init` / `prompt` / `followup` / `approve` / `reject` run on the
   452	 *    serial queue, preserving arrival order (init still completes before a
   453	 *    back-to-back prompt).
   454	 *  - CONTROL plane: `cancel`, stdin EOF and SIGINT/SIGTERM PREEMPT — they invoke
   455	 *    `target.cancel()` / `target.shutdown()` DIRECTLY, bypassing the serial
   456	 *    queue, even while a data task is in flight. Because `cancel`/`shutdown`
   457	 *    abort the session, they are exactly what unblocks a stuck prompt. Signals
   458	 *    additionally arm a hard {@link ControlPlaneOptions.forceExitMs} deadline: if
   459	 *    graceful teardown (or the final flush) stalls, the process is force-exited.
   460	 */
